+### Local Illumination Model
+A simplified illumination model that evaluates only direct illumination from point lights, discarding shadow casting and indirect light [S2, unofficial].
+1.  Replace hemispherical integral with a summation over $n$ point lights [S2, unofficial].
+2.  For each light, compute irradiance using inverse-square law $\Phi / (4\pi |x - x_s|^2)$ [S2, unofficial].
+3.  Multiply by BRDF $f_r$ and cosine term $\cos \theta_s$ [S2, unofficial].
+4.  Sum contributions and add emitted radiance $L_e$ [S2, unofficial].
+Formula:
+$$ L_0(x, \vec{\omega}) = L_e(x, \vec{\omega}) + \sum_{s=0}^n \frac{\Phi}{4\pi |x - x_s|^2} f_r(\vec{\omega}, \vec{\omega}_s, x) * \cos \theta_s $$
+where $\Phi$ is light flux, $n$ is number of point lights, $x_s$ is light position, and $\theta_s$ is angle between light direction and surface normal [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The concept of shading isosurfaces derived from scalar fields originated with the development of ray casting techniques for medical and scientific visualization in the late 1980s and early 1990s. Early methods relied on simple Phong shading using finite-difference normals [S3, unofficial]. The use of higher-order derivatives (Hessian, curvature) for shading was introduced to enhance the perception of surface shape and internal structures, with deferred shading approaches becoming prominent in the 2000s as GPU capabilities allowed for multi-pass rendering [S1, unofficial]. Matcap texturing for implicit surfaces has been adapted from polygonal rendering techniques more recently [S1, unofficial].
+The concept of shading isosurfaces extracted from volume data dates back to the early days of volume visualization, where Phong shading was applied to triangle meshes generated by Marching Cubes [S3, unofficial]. Ray casting methods adopted similar local lighting models to provide depth cues [S3, unofficial]. Deferred shading techniques for isosurfaces were introduced to allow for more complex differential property analysis (curvature, flow) without the performance penalty of computing them for every sample along the ray [S1, unofficial]. Color enhancement techniques leveraging scalar changing rates were developed to provide volumetric context without the cost of full volume rendering [S1, unofficial]. Matcap texturing for implicit surfaces was adapted from polygonal rendering techniques [S1, unofficial].
 
